.TH CVEC_INIT 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_init \- create the vector behind a declared handle (fatal on failure)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_init(v);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro creates a vector and stores it in v. cvec_declare(3) or
cvec_declare_scoped(3) must declare v first. The element size comes from
the element type in that declaration, so this macro repeats nothing about
the type. The macro creates the vector through the default allocator.
cvec_init_mp(3) is the form that takes an allocator of its own.

cvec_construct(3) puts this step into the declaration. Use the declare step
and the initialize step separately when the two cannot sit on one line. One
example is a choice between the default allocator and a custom one that
comes further down the function.

The new vector holds no elements. It starts at the minimum capacity of 4.
The capacity doubles when a push finds the buffer full. It halves when the
element count falls below a quarter of it. It never falls below that
minimum of 4.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v  Name of the vector variable for the new vector. It must be a plain
   identifier. cvec_declare(3) or cvec_declare_scoped(3) must declare it
   first, and its companion type variables must still be in scope.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to a statement, not to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() when it cannot
create the vector. The message names v and the error below it. Use
cvector_create(3) directly to handle a failed creation yourself. That
function does not stop the program.
The element size comes from the declared element type. A handle with
companion type variables that are out of scope therefore does not
compile.
The macro expands to a do/while block. It needs a semicolon at the end of
the call site.
The macro evaluates v more than once, and it pastes v into the names of
the companion variables. v must therefore be a plain identifier, and
never an expression.
Free a vector from this macro with cvec_destroy(3).
cvec_declare_scoped(3) makes that call automatically at the end of the
scope.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_declare(my_vec, int);
cvec_init(my_vec);
cvec_push(my_vec, 42);
cvec_destroy(my_vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_init_mp (3),
.BR cvec_declare (3),
.BR cvec_declare_scoped (3),
.BR cvec_construct (3),
.BR cvec_destroy (3),
.BR cvector_create (3)
